        "title": "Medieval and Islamic Philosophy - Augustine, Avicenna, Averroes, Aquinas, Maimonides",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nGREEK PHILOSOPHY\n(600 BCE – 200 CE)\n         │\n         │ [Western Roman Empire collapses, 476 CE]\n         │ [Eastern Roman/Byzantine Empire continues]\n         │\n         v\nSYRIAC TRANSLATION MOVEMENT         AUGUSTINE (354–430 CE)\n(4th–7th centuries)                 Western synthesis:\nGreek texts → Syriac                Plato + Neoplatonism + Christianity\n         │\n         │ [Islamic conquests, 7th century]\n         │\n         v\nARABIC TRANSLATION MOVEMENT         ISLAMIC GOLDEN AGE\n(8th–10th centuries, Baghdad)       Avicenna (980–1037)\nGreek texts → Arabic                Averroes (1126–1198)\n+ Original Islamic philosophy       Al-Farabi, Al-Kindi, Al-Ghazali\n         │\n         │ [Reconquista; Toledo translation schools, 12th century]\n         │\n         v\nLATIN SCHOLASTICISM                 JEWISH SYNTHESIS\n(11th–14th centuries)               Maimonides (1138–1204)\nArabic + Greek → Latin              Aristotle + Torah\nAquinas (1225–1274)\n         │\n         v\nEARLY MODERN PHILOSOPHY\n(1600 onward)\n```"
